6136-2020-ER-F0076 P3 1 p134 The performance was slightly moderate, few candidates performed well. Most of the candidates scored average marks and below. The question paper covered the whole syllabus thoroughly with clear instructions and simple English. Individual questions were well structured with clear and concise instructions. The application of knowledge is lacking in most candidates, e.g. they know the data collection methods but they cannot explain how these methods are used. The question paper could not be compared to any, since candidates took the examination for the first time.
6136-2020-ER-F0080 P3 1(a)(iv) p134 (iv) Fairly answered, the majority of the candidates were copying from the survey instead of identifying solutions to baby dumping.
6136-2020-ER-F0083 P3 1(b)(ii) p134 (ii) Fairly answered as many candidates failed to give the advantages of a questionnaire instead they confused the advantages of a questionnaire with the sampling methods.
6136-2020-ER-F0084 P3 1(b)(iii) p134 (iii) Poorly answered, the majority of the candidates could not design a questionnaire following the guidelines rather they gave random questions that are not well constructed.
6136-2020-ER-F0085 P3 1(c)(i) p135 (c) (i) Fairly answered, some candidates could not explain what random sampling is.
6136-2020-ER-F0086 P3 1(c)(ii) p135 (ii) Fairly answered, some candidates confused sample with sampling methods.
6136-2020-ER-F0087 P3 1(c)(iii) p135 (iii) Poorly answered, the majority of the candidates repeated random sampling and some were able to give the method but could not give the reasons why it was suitable.
6136-2020-ER-F0088 P3 1(c)(iv) p135 (iv) Poorly answered, candidates were giving advantages of a questionnaire and define sampling instead of giving reasons for sampling.
6136-2020-ER-F0092 P3 2(d) p136 (d) Poorly answered as the majority of the candidates could not title the graph, give the key, calculate the degrees, use compass and protractor for accurate plotting.
6136-2020-ER-F0093 P3 3(a) p136 3 (a) Well answered, although few candidates could not come up with developmental topics, instead they wrote general topics, some repeated baby dumping in Ohangwena and Oshana regions. Some candidates failed to differentiate between a hypothesis and the research question. Some could not come up with well formulated aims. Some candidates surprisingly produced research topics that are not developmental issues such as: • car accidents • traffic counts • development • smoking • poverty • stress • river processes
6136-2020-ER-F0094 P3 3(b) p136 (b) Poorly answered, the majority of candidates defined a pilot study instead of explaining how a pilot study was used and why researchers need to use a pilot study before the main research.
6136-2020-ER-F0095 P3 3(c) p136 (c) Poorly answered, most of the candidates could name the methods but could not explain how such methods could be used to collect data. They opted to definitions of the methods.
6136-2020-ER-F0096 P3 3(d) p137 (d) Poorly answered, the majority of the candidates gave problems they encountered during the research instead of problems encountered using interviews. Some wrote problems without solutions and others gave solutions without problems. Candidates were expected to give problems and solutions.
6136-2021-ER-F0099 P1 1 p115 The overall performance of the learners was slightly poor compared to last year. This is because most learners could not interpret questions correctly. They also failed to recall basic definitions, which is an indication of poor content mastery. Most learners could not comprehend the use of command words, i.e. two opposite statements were required to answer a question on which learners were asked to differentiate. In addition, it was observed that most learners do not read instructions, they could not start each question on a separate page and they wrote between margins which made it difficult for the markers to indicate marks. Learners also failed to number their answers correctly; they instead used figures and page numbers, which should be discouraged.
6136-2021-ER-F0101 P1 1(a)(ii) p115 (ii) Poorly answered. Most learners could only refer to the role of men in general without referring to the source.
6136-2021-ER-F0102 P1 1(a)(iii) p115 (iii) Poorly answered. Most learners could only list basic human rights instead of the characteristics of democracy.
6136-2021-ER-F0103 P1 1(b) p115 (b) Fairly answered, however some learners only stated the general daily activities of councillors, e.g. to listen to people’s problems.
6136-2021-ER-F0105 P1 1(c)(ii) p115 (ii) Fairly answered. Some learners confused the market economy with the mixed economy.
6136-2021-ER-F0107 P1 1(d)(i) p116 (d) (i) Poorly answered. Learners could not differentiate between the two types of decision making and the majority were clueless about the two decision making bodies.
6136-2021-ER-F0108 P1 1(d)(ii) p116 (ii) Poorly answered. Most learners failed to give the advantages of the decision making process.
6136-2021-ER-F0109 P1 2(a)(i) p116 2 (a) (i) Fairly answered. Some learners failed to give two opposite statements and they lost marks in this regard.
6136-2021-ER-F0111 P1 2(b)(i) p116 (b) (i) Fairly answered. Some learners confused communal ownership with public ownership.
6136-2021-ER-F0113 P1 2(c) p116 (c) Fairly answered. Learners could only refer to private ownership instead of how the types of ownership influence the wealth of a country.
6136-2021-ER-F0114 P1 2(d)(i) p116 (d) (i) Poorly answered. The majority of the learners confused the methods of production with the types of technology.
6136-2021-ER-F0115 P1 2(d)(ii) p116 (ii) Fairly answered. Some learners failed to identify the methods of production in Fig. 4A, but only referred to the types of technology.
6136-2021-ER-F0116 P1 2(e) p117 (e) Poorly answered. Learners could only refer to problems of urban areas.
6136-2021-ER-F0119 P1 3(a)(iii) p117 (iii) Fairly answered, however, some learners failed to specify the push and pull factors.
