6136-2023-ER-F0361 P3 1 p164 The application of knowledge is lacking in most candidates, e.g. they know the methods of sampling but cannot explain how these methods are used and why. There are still more centres where candidates scored less or no marks in section B and it is evident that some teachers did not guide the learners properly in carrying out the research. Teachers must refrain from giving one topic to the whole centre. Candidate scripts were generally neat, English language grammar hampers them in expressing themselves fully.
6136-2023-ER-F0363 P3 1(a)(ii) p164 (ii) Poorly answered, the majority of candidates copied directly from the source instead of using their own words.
6136-2023-ER-F0364 P3 1(a)(iii) p164 (iii) Poorly answered, the majority of the candidates could not apply how observation was used to collect data. Many just defined what is observation. The majority could not name the recording method rather gave recording methods of an interview.
6136-2023-ER-F0365 P3 1(a)(iv) p164 (iv) Fairly answered, most candidates managed to identify the difficulties of the observation method but could not explain or provide the matching solutions.
6136-2023-ER-F0366 P3 1(b)(i) p165 (b) (i) Fairly answered, most of the candidates scored one mark, most answered it is cheap instead of stating it is cheap and efficient, collect large amount of information without including in a short period of time. Most candidates gave primary source while they could easily use first-hand information. Some gave advantages of other data collection methods.
6136-2023-ER-F0367 P3 1(b)(ii) p165 (ii) Fairly answered. Most candidates were able to give the difficulties of observation but could not explain or provide the matching solutions. Some candidates gave difficulties of interviews and observation such as time consuming and people being suspicious. Some gave wrong solutions of difficulties like not good for illiterate people and take a translator as a solution.
6136-2023-ER-F0368 P3 1(b)(iii) p165 (iii) Fairly answered, the majority scored marks for the layout, but most of the candidates did not have an understanding of “quality of life”. Some candidates were not able to formulate relevant questions. Some candidates ended up drawing things such as houses, health workers,hospitals and villages.
6136-2023-ER-F0370 P3 1(c)(ii) p165 (ii) Fairly answered, most candidates could draw a graph, but could not do the scaling, labelling, title and plotting properly.
6136-2023-ER-F0371 P3 1(c)(iii) p166 (iii) Poorly answered, candidates could not draw a conclusion on the information given, many wrote findings.
6136-2023-ER-F0377 P3 2(c)(ii) p166 (ii) Poorly answered, candidates could not explain how students can enusre that information taken from the internet is accurate. The majority gave the disadvantages of using secondary data.
6136-2023-ER-F0378 P3 2(c)(iii) p166 (iii) Well answered, the majority gave relevant answers on how to reduce urbanisation, however, few failed to develop create jobs where they are expected to state how it should be done. Few repeated answers such as provide services and provide electricity, clean water which are in one line.
6136-2023-ER-F0379 P3 3(a)(i) p167 3 (a) (i) Fairly answered, candidates could not identify a researchable problem with an area.
6136-2023-ER-F0382 P3 3(a)(iii) p167 (iii) Poorly answered, the majority could not link their aims to the research questions or hypothesis. Take note that the aims are derived from the research questions or hypothesis.
6136-2023-ER-F0383 P3 3(b) p167 (b) Fairly answered, candidates were able to identify the sampling methods and provide advantages, however, the advantages were mixed with other sampling methods. Furthermore, candidates failed to score marks on the application on how such sampling methods was used to obtain the number required. Sample size and population were not provided in the explanation, therefore, candidates could not score marks.
6136-2023-ER-F0384 P3 3(c) p168 (c) Poorly answered, candidates could not draw conclusions but instead, they gave findings not supported by evidence. Candidate could not give the general trend and use words such as majority, most and least. Some provided conclusions that were not related to the stated aims and topic.
6136-2023-ER-F0386 P3 3(d) p168 (d) Fairly answered, the majority of the candidates were giving recommendations without providing the implementers. Most still use the word Government as an implementer instead of specific authority. Some candidates could not link recommendations to the problem investigated.
